import{_ as t,o,c as d,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.BM5YPQmL.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Guard","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"concepts/guard.md","filePath":"concepts/guard.md","lastUpdated":1793784412000}'),s={name:"concepts/guard.md"};function a(n,e,c,i,l,h){return o(),d("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="guard" tabindex="-1">Guard <a class="header-anchor" href="#guard" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Guard&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The guard is everything that stands between an applet and the user. Some of it is kehto&#39;s and kernl turns it on; some of it is kernl&#39;s and runs in front of kehto&#39;s bridge. The user sees it as one list of mitigations with reasons. This page is that list, with the numbers.</p><p>The rule from <code>SOUL.md</code>: if kehto ships a mitigation, kernl enables it by default and documents how to loosen it. The hosted shell boots <code>strict</code>, and there only the user loosens it (<a href="./../decisions#d-003">D-003</a>). An embedding host may choose the starting profile (<code>KernlOptions.guard.profile</code>), which changes limits only; the sandbox, the CSP, the frame lock and consent are fixed. In this draft the profile is shell-wide and resets to <code>strict</code> on every boot; per-applet profiles are planned (<a href="./../roadmap#_6-guard">Roadmap</a>).</p><p><strong>What refuses, what reports, what stops.</strong> For an applet&#39;s port traffic, which is everything after the handshake, kehto&#39;s runtime firewall refuses an envelope before dispatch when it goes over the per-class rates kernl installs at mount, or over kehto&#39;s own startup burst. kernl&#39;s own layers refuse before dispatch too. Its ingress guard and focus firewall see each envelope synchronously, just before kehto dispatches it; they cannot hold it, but whenever one of them will not let it through (an oversized request, a burst above kernl&#39;s per-second rate, a request over the smaller unfocused budget, or any non-control request from a hidden window) kernl has the runtime firewall refuse that one dispatch with <code>refuseCurrentDispatch</code>. The applet gets a <code>firewall:</code> error and can retry; a hidden window can retry once it is visible. Control envelopes (<code>shell.*</code>, <code>inc.*</code>, <code>theme.*</code>, closes and the other <code>control</code> classes) are never refused, so a window always stays controllable. Every refusal is still a guard event. Quarantine goes further. When an envelope crosses the flood threshold, or a hidden window&#39;s download crosses its allowance, kernl revokes the binding inside the observation, so that envelope is not dispatched and the applet is closed (<a href="#where-enforcement-happens">Where enforcement happens</a>). The visibility gate holds only the window-level <code>hello</code>. Signing consent is a third path, in the signer itself.</p><h2 id="the-eleven-mitigations" tabindex="-1">The eleven mitigations <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-eleven-mitigations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The eleven mitigations&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>describeMitigations(config)</code> in <code>@kernl/guard</code> returns this list as <code>MitigationDescription[]</code>, in this order, with the <code>limit</code> text computed from the profile in <code>config</code>. The shell renders it verbatim; the titles below are the ones the user sees, and they carry no protocol vocabulary. The third column is for developers and does.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th><code>id</code></th><th>Title shown to the user</th><th>What it protects against</th><th><code>provider</code></th><th><code>limit</code> in <code>strict</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td><code>zero-network-sandbox</code></td><td>Applets cannot reach the network on their own</td><td>Exfiltration, tracking pixels, loading code the user did not verify. The frame loads through <code>srcdoc</code> with <code>sandbox=&quot;allow-scripts&quot;</code> and the CSP below, and kehto&#39;s prelude removes WebRTC before applet code runs. kernl refuses applet markup that declares a frame, and its frame lock stops applet code from creating one, so no child realm with a working peer connection can exist (<a href="#no-network-path">No network path</a>, <a href="./../decisions#d-014">D-014</a>). Every byte from the network arrives through a shell service.</td><td><code>both</code></td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><code>bound-identity</code></td><td>An applet is only trusted while its connection and code match</td><td>A reloaded or substituted document inheriting grants, or one frame speaking for another. Identity is <code>(dTag, artifactHash)</code> plus the one <code>MessagePort</code> transferred in <code>napplet.hello</code>. Any other window-level message, a second <code>hello</code>, a <code>hello</code> without exactly one port, a second load or a closed port revokes the binding.</td><td><code>kehto</code></td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><code>verified-artifacts</code></td><td>Applet code is verified before it runs, and again before reuse</td><td>A gateway or cache serving different code than the signed manifest names. Bytes are fetched by hash through <code>@kehto/nip/5d</code>, cached only after verification and re-verified on every read.</td><td><code>kehto</code></td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><code>replay-detection</code></td><td>Old or repeated posts are not sent</td><td>The same signed post sent twice, or one dated to slip past a time window. kehto&#39;s replay detector runs on <code>relay.publish</code> after the shell signs: it refuses an event dated more than the replay window (30 s by default) in the past, more than 10 s in the future, or whose id it already saw inside the window. <code>outbox.publish</code> is not checked. A message from a window other than the applet&#39;s own is a port-binding matter, covered by <code>bound-identity</code>.</td><td><code>kehto</code></td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><code>rate-limits</code></td><td>Applets have a budget for how often they can act</td><td>Publishing, subscribing or spending quota at a rate no human interaction explains. Refused before dispatch: kehto&#39;s runtime firewall refuses an over-budget envelope until the allowance refills, using the per-class rates kernl installs at mount, where deletions count against <code>relay:write</code>. The same firewall applies kehto&#39;s startup burst guard: more than 20 operations in a window&#39;s first 3 s are refused. kernl&#39;s focus firewall counts the classes again as each envelope arrives, including a separate <code>relay:publish:delete</code> bucket, and reports overruns as <code>rate:&lt;class&gt;</code> events.</td><td><code>both</code></td><td><code>publish 20 per 60s, delete 5 per 60s, messages 20 per 60s</code></td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><code>focus-tightening</code></td><td>Applets you are not using are measured against a smaller budget</td><td>Background windows spending a foreground budget. Refused before dispatch: kernl&#39;s focus firewall counts an unfocused window at <code>unfocusedMultiplier</code> of capacity as each envelope arrives; one over it emits a guard event (<code>rate:&lt;class&gt;</code>) and kernl has the runtime firewall refuse that dispatch. Once the window is focused, the full budget applies again. Focus is observed by the shell, never self-reported. The runtime firewall itself applies no unfocused multiplier: kehto&#39;s <code>ShellAdapter</code> does not forward <code>getFocusContext</code> (<a href="./../alpha-status#upstream-gaps">upstream gaps 1 and 4</a>), so the tightening is kernl&#39;s. The rate-limits budget above still applies in full.</td><td><code>kernl</code></td><td><code>25% of the focused budget</code></td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><code>hidden-deferral</code></td><td>Hidden applets cannot act until you can see them</td><td>Hidden or minimised applets acting where the user cannot see them. While a window is not visible, every non-control port envelope is refused before dispatch (<code>hidden:&lt;class&gt;</code>), not held: the applet gets a <code>firewall:</code> error and can retry when visible. Control envelopes are answered. Each <code>resource.*</code> request from a hidden window counts against <code>hiddenResourceAllowance</code> as it arrives; the one past it quarantines the window (<code>flood:hidden-resource</code>): the binding is revoked inside the observation, so that request is not dispatched, and the frame is removed. Quarantine is checked first, so a request past the allowance quarantines rather than being refused. The visibility gate holds only a hidden window&#39;s window-level <code>hello</code>.</td><td><code>kernl</code></td><td><code>8 downloads while hidden</code></td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><code>ingress-flood</code></td><td>Applets that flood the shell are put in quarantine</td><td>A busy loop starving the shell&#39;s main thread. Per-window token bucket, counted as each port envelope arrives: non-control envelopes in a burst above <code>ingressBurst</code> are refused before dispatch (<code>flood:burst</code>, <code>flood:queue-full</code>) and are not queued; control envelopes still pass. The message that reaches <code>quarantineThreshold</code> in 10 s quarantines the window: the binding is revoked inside the observation, so that message is not dispatched, and the frame is removed. Only the window-level <code>hello</code> is actually queued or rejected.</td><td><code>kernl</code></td><td><code>8 messages per second, 300 per 10s</code></td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><code>size-caps</code></td><td>Oversized requests are refused</td><td>Memory pressure from oversized filters or payloads. The ingress guard measures <code>resource.*</code> and <code>relay.subscribe</code>/<code>outbox.subscribe</code> envelopes as they arrive and rejects an oversized one with <code>size:&lt;class&gt;</code>. kehto&#39;s runtime cannot measure these itself (it sizes only an envelope&#39;s <code>event</code>), so kernl calls <code>refuseCurrentDispatch</code>, which sets the applet&#39;s runtime policy to <code>deny</code> for that one synchronous dispatch and restores it in a microtask. The applet receives a <code>firewall:</code> error, nothing is fetched, and the user sees the guard event. An oversized <code>hello</code> is dropped.</td><td><code>both</code></td><td><code>downloads 64 KiB, queries 256 KiB</code></td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><code>device-denial</code></td><td>Applets cannot use your camera, microphone or other devices</td><td>An applet reaching hardware. <code>denyNappletControllerAccess</code> gives the frame the Permissions Policy <code>gamepad &#39;none&#39;; hid &#39;none&#39;; usb &#39;none&#39;</code>; the frame&#39;s <code>allow</code> attribute delegates nothing else, so camera, microphone and other powerful features stay off. There is no grant for any of them.</td><td><code>kehto</code></td><td>none</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td><code>destructive-consent</code></td><td>Deleting or overwriting asks you first</td><td>An applet acquiring authority the user did not knowingly give. <code>createSigningConsent</code> asks <code>host.presentConsent</code> before a destructive kind (every replaceable kind 0, 3, 10000–19999; every addressable kind 30000–39999; 5; 62) is signed for an applet, after looking up the current version in the store and then on the outbox and discovery relays for up to <code>CURRENT_VERSION_TIMEOUT_MS</code> (4000 ms); if none is found the summary says &quot;Current version: could not be loaded; this replaces it entirely&quot;. It runs on both routes: the window signer from <code>ShellAdapter.auth.getSigner(windowId)</code> for <code>relay.publish</code>, and kernl&#39;s outbox gate for <code>outbox.publish</code>. A denial is <code>denied:consent</code> and nothing is signed; answers are not remembered, and the shell&#39;s own signing is not gated. kehto&#39;s runtime has no such step.</td><td><code>kernl</code></td><td>none</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>enabled</code> is <code>true</code> for every row in every profile; the field exists for the UI contract. Two things are not separate rows: the opaque-origin frame, the CSP and the frame lock are one row (<code>zero-network-sandbox</code>), and kehto&#39;s startup burst guard is part of <code>rate-limits</code>, because the same runtime firewall applies both (<a href="#layers">What <code>burstGuard</code> is for</a>). See <code>mitigations.feature</code>, scenario &quot;every mitigation is described once, in display order&quot;.</p><h2 id="operation-classes" tabindex="-1">Operation classes <a class="header-anchor" href="#operation-classes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Operation classes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>kehto&#39;s firewall treats the operation class as an opaque key. <code>classifyEnvelope</code> gives each inbound envelope one of eleven classes from its <code>type</code> and, for publishes, its <code>kind</code>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Envelope type</th><th>Class</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>relay.publish</code>, <code>relay.publishEncrypted</code> with <code>kind: 5</code></td><td><code>relay:publish:delete</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>relay.publish</code>, <code>relay.publishEncrypted</code> (any other kind)</td><td><code>relay:publish</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>relay.subscribe</code></td><td><code>relay:subscribe</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>outbox.subscribe</code>, <code>outbox.query</code>, <code>outbox.getEvent</code></td><td><code>outbox:subscribe</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>outbox.publish</code> (any kind, including 5)</td><td><code>outbox:publish</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>resource.*</code></td><td><code>resource:bytes</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>storage.set</code>, <code>storage.remove</code></td><td><code>storage:write</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>dm.send</code></td><td><code>dm:send</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>intent.invoke</code>, <code>intent.open</code></td><td><code>intent:invoke</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>relay.close</code>, <code>outbox.close</code>, <code>storage.get</code>, <code>storage.keys</code>, <code>shell.*</code>, <code>inc.*</code>, <code>theme.*</code>, <code>identity.*</code>, other <code>dm.*</code></td><td><code>control</code></td></tr><tr><td>anything else (<code>clipboard.write</code>, …)</td><td><code>generic</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>relay:publish:delete</code> gives kind 5 deletions their own, tighter bucket in kernl&#39;s focus firewall, so a burst of deletes is refused as <code>rate:relay:publish:delete</code>. The runtime&#39;s own rates have no separate delete bucket: kehto&#39;s runtime firewall keys by capability, and deletions share the <code>relay:write</code> budget with other publishes (<a href="#where-enforcement-happens">translation table</a>). What stops an unwanted deletion is signing consent, not the rate. See <code>classification.feature</code>.</p><h2 id="profiles" tabindex="-1">Profiles <a class="header-anchor" href="#profiles" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Profiles&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A profile is a <code>GuardLimits</code> record plus the derived kehto <code>FirewallConfig</code>; <code>createGuardConfig(profile)</code> builds both. Profiles change limits only. <code>GUARD_PROFILES</code> lists them tightest first.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Limit</th><th><code>strict</code> (default)</th><th><code>balanced</code></th><th><code>relaxed</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>ingressBurst</code> (messages per second before queueing)</td><td>8</td><td>12</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td><code>ingressQueue</code> (queued messages before rejection)</td><td>64</td><td>128</td><td>192</td></tr><tr><td><code>quarantineThreshold</code> (messages per rolling 10 s before quarantine)</td><td>300</td><td>420</td><td>800</td></tr><tr><td><code>hiddenResourceAllowance</code> (resource requests while hidden before quarantine)</td><td>8</td><td>16</td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td><code>deferQueue</code> (held envelopes while hidden before rejection)</td><td>200</td><td>400</td><td>600</td></tr><tr><td><code>maxResourceEnvelopeBytes</code></td><td>64 KiB (65 536)</td><td>128 KiB (131 072)</td><td>192 KiB (196 608)</td></tr><tr><td><code>maxSubscribeEnvelopeBytes</code></td><td>256 KiB (262 144)</td><td>512 KiB (524 288)</td><td>768 KiB (786 432)</td></tr><tr><td><code>unfocusedMultiplier</code> (rate capacity while unfocused)</td><td>0.25</td><td>0.5</td><td>0.75</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The per-class <code>rates</code> are a token-bucket capacity per window. <code>balanced</code> is exactly 2× <code>strict</code> and <code>relaxed</code> exactly 3×; the windows do not change.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Window</th><th><code>strict</code></th><th><code>balanced</code></th><th><code>relaxed</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>relay:subscribe</code></td><td>60 s</td><td>30</td><td>60</td><td>90</td></tr><tr><td><code>relay:publish</code></td><td>60 s</td><td>20</td><td>40</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td><code>relay:publish:delete</code></td><td>60 s</td><td>5</td><td>10</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td><code>outbox:subscribe</code></td><td>60 s</td><td>30</td><td>60</td><td>90</td></tr><tr><td><code>outbox:publish</code></td><td>60 s</td><td>20</td><td>40</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td><code>resource:bytes</code></td><td>10 s</td><td>120</td><td>240</td><td>360</td></tr><tr><td><code>storage:write</code></td><td>10 s</td><td>60</td><td>120</td><td>180</td></tr><tr><td><code>dm:send</code></td><td>60 s</td><td>20</td><td>40</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td><code>intent:invoke</code></td><td>60 s</td><td>10</td><td>20</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td><code>control</code></td><td>10 s</td><td>120</td><td>240</td><td>360</td></tr><tr><td><code>generic</code></td><td>10 s</td><td>120</td><td>240</td><td>360</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Every rate kernl registers in kehto has exceed action <code>block</code>; kehto&#39;s own default would be <code>flag</code>. The shell boots in <code>strict</code>. <a href="./../how-tos/change-guard-profile">Change the guard profile</a> shows how a user changes the shell-wide profile; per-applet profiles are planned. See <code>profiles.feature</code>.</p><h2 id="layers" tabindex="-1">Layers <a class="header-anchor" href="#layers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Layers&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Three stateful objects, each built from the same <code>GuardConfig</code>, each keyed by <code>windowId</code>, each with a <code>forget(windowId)</code> to call on revocation. This section describes each layer&#39;s own API, which is what a caller that sits in front of dispatch gets. In the composed shell that is true only for the window-level <code>hello</code>; for port traffic the dispositions below become events and, for <code>quarantine</code>, a revocation that also stops the envelope that triggered it (<a href="#where-enforcement-happens">Where enforcement happens</a>).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Enforces</th><th>Factory</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ingress guard</td><td><code>ingressBurst</code>, <code>ingressQueue</code>, <code>quarantineThreshold</code>, <code>hiddenResourceAllowance</code>, both envelope size caps</td><td><code>createIngressGuard({ config, now?, onEvent?, drainBatch? })</code></td></tr><tr><td>Visibility gate</td><td><code>deferQueue</code>; which classes wait while hidden</td><td><code>createVisibilityGate({ config, now?, onEvent?, nappletFor? })</code></td></tr><tr><td>Focus firewall</td><td>kehto <code>evaluate()</code>: per-applet class rates, the <code>generic</code> default rate, the content matchers, <code>unfocusedMultiplier</code>, per-applet policies</td><td><code>createFocusFirewall({ config, now?, onEvent? })</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Ingress guard.</strong> Each window has a token bucket of <code>ingressBurst</code> per second. While tokens remain, <code>admit</code> returns <code>pass</code>. When they are spent, or while anything is already queued, the envelope is queued and <code>admit</code> returns <code>defer</code> with reason <code>flood:burst</code>; nothing overtakes the queue. Past <code>ingressQueue</code> the envelope is rejected with <code>flood:queue-full</code>. With <code>admit(..., { hold: false })</code>, which the pipeline uses for port traffic it refuses rather than holds, a spent burst still returns <code>defer</code> with <code>flood:burst</code> but nothing is queued. <code>drain(windowId)</code> releases at most <code>drainBatch</code> (default 4) queued envelopes, never more than the refilled tokens allow, ordered <code>control</code> first, then <code>resource:bytes</code>, then everything else, oldest first within a rank. Arrivals are counted over a rolling <code>QUARANTINE_WINDOW_MS</code> (10 s); the arrival that reaches <code>quarantineThreshold</code> quarantines the window with <code>flood:quarantine</code>, drops its queue, and every later envelope returns <code>quarantine</code> with the same reason until <code>release(windowId)</code> (fresh budget, quarantine lifted) or <code>forget(windowId)</code>. While a window is hidden, each <code>resource:bytes</code> envelope counts against <code>hiddenResourceAllowance</code>; the one past it quarantines with <code>flood:hidden-resource</code>. Any admission while visible resets that count. An envelope whose class has a size cap and whose <code>size</code> exceeds it is rejected with <code>size:&lt;class&gt;</code>; an envelope without a <code>size</code> is not size-checked. A clock that runs backwards grants no tokens. See <code>ingress.feature</code>.</p><p><strong>Visibility gate.</strong> The shell reports visibility with <code>setVisibility</code>; a window it has not reported on counts as visible and unfocused. <code>shouldDefer(windowId, opClass)</code> is true when the window is hidden and the class is not <code>control</code>, so a hidden window can still be closed, themed or asked for its identity. <code>defer</code> queues up to <code>deferQueue</code> envelopes and then returns <code>false</code> while emitting a <code>reject</code> event with reason <code>defer:queue-full</code>; the event names the applet through <code>nappletFor</code>, or <code>unknown</code> without one. <code>drain</code> returns every held envelope oldest first and empties the queue. Deferral is a privacy and resource measure, not a rate limit: a minimised applet should not fetch or publish while the user cannot see it. See <code>visibility.feature</code>.</p><p><strong>Focus firewall.</strong> <code>observe</code> builds a kehto <code>Observation</code> from the envelope and the shell&#39;s <code>WindowVisibility</code> (<code>focused</code>, and <code>msSinceFocusGain</code> when known) and runs <code>evaluate()</code>. On the first observation of an applet it calls <code>applyNappletRules</code> so the applet&#39;s class rates exist in kehto&#39;s per-applet map; kehto keys those budgets by applet (<code>dTag</code>), so two windows of the same applet share a budget (<code>focus-firewall.feature</code>, scenario &quot;budgets are per applet, not per window&quot;). Decisions map to events: <code>reject</code> emits <code>reject</code>; <code>pass</code> with action <code>flag</code> emits <code>flag</code>; <code>prompt</code> emits <code>reject</code> with reason <code>prompt:&lt;ruleId&gt;</code>. kernl treats a prompt as a denial here because the layer has no UI; signing consent for destructive writes is a separate path, in the signer wrapper, not in the firewall. kehto&#39;s <code>rate:*</code> rule ids name the tier (<code>rate:opclass</code>, <code>rate:global</code>, <code>rate:default</code>); the event reason is rewritten to <code>rate:&lt;class&gt;</code> so the user sees what ran out. Other rule ids pass through unchanged, for example <code>matcher:size:resource:bytes</code> and <code>policy:deny</code>. In the composed shell a <code>matcher:size:*</code> event cannot occur: the ingress guard applies the same caps first, and the pipeline runs the focus firewall only for envelopes the ingress guard passed. <code>forget(windowId)</code> drops the buckets of every applet seen in that window, which also resets the budget of another window running the same applet; revocation is user-driven, so that is accepted.</p><p><strong>What <code>burstGuard</code> is for.</strong> <code>createGuardConfig</code> sets <code>firewall.burstGuard</code> to <code>{ windowMs: 1000, maxOps: ingressBurst, action: &#39;block&#39; }</code>. kehto&#39;s init-burst guard only fires for callers that supply <code>initElapsedMs</code> in the observation, and the focus firewall never does, so this field never fires inside <code>@kernl/guard</code>. The guard that does fire is the runtime&#39;s: kehto&#39;s runtime supplies <code>initElapsedMs</code> (time since the window&#39;s session registered) and evaluates its own configuration, kehto&#39;s default of 20 operations in the first 3 s with action <code>block</code>, because kernl installs rates in the runtime and nothing else. An applet that sends more than 20 envelopes in its first 3 s has the excess refused with a <code>firewall:</code> error.</p><h2 id="where-enforcement-happens" tabindex="-1">Where enforcement happens <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-enforcement-happens" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where enforcement happens&quot;">​</a></h2><p>kehto&#39;s bridge owns the <code>MessagePort</code> an applet speaks over and shows the host that traffic only through <code>ShellAdapter.onNappletMessage</code>. kehto&#39;s <code>dispatchEnvelope</code> calls that hook synchronously for each envelope, before it resolves the window&#39;s session and hands the envelope to the runtime, and ignores the hook&#39;s answer. kernl therefore observes every envelope before dispatch but cannot hold or drop it (<a href="./../decisions#d-001">D-001</a>). Enforcement is layered (<a href="./../decisions#d-012">D-012</a>):</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Traffic</th><th>Where</th><th>How</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The window-level <code>napplet.hello</code></td><td>kernl, before kehto sees it</td><td><code>Kernl.handleWindowMessage</code> resolves the sending window from <code>originRegistry</code>, never from the message, and runs ingress guard → visibility gate → focus firewall before <code>bridge.handleMessage</code>. This is the one message the pipeline can hold or drop; any other window-level message from a bound applet is kehto&#39;s <code>window-message</code> revocation.</td></tr><tr><td>Port traffic, judged</td><td>kernl, inside <code>onNappletMessage</code>, before dispatch</td><td><code>onNappletMessage</code> feeds <code>GuardPipeline.observe</code>: the ingress guard and the focus firewall advance their counters and emit events, and <code>observe</code> returns a disposition (see the next rows for what a <code>defer</code> or <code>reject</code> does). A <code>quarantine</code> disposition calls <code>onQuarantine</code>, which marks the handle revoked with reason <code>quarantine</code> and calls <code>bridge.revokeNapplet(windowId, &#39;host&#39;)</code> while kehto is still inside the hook. kehto then finds no session for the window and does not dispatch the envelope, and the frame is removed.</td></tr><tr><td>Port traffic, refused by rate</td><td>kehto&#39;s runtime firewall, configured by kernl</td><td><code>kernl.mount</code> calls <code>applyRuntimeFirewallRules(bridge.runtime.firewallState, config, dTag)</code>, which installs the profile&#39;s per-class rates and global rate for that applet. It sets no policy: kehto&#39;s <code>allow</code> policy would make <code>evaluate()</code> skip every rate, matcher and burst check. The runtime refuses over-budget envelopes, and envelopes past its startup burst, itself, before it handles them, and answers the applet with an error that starts <code>firewall:</code>. Rates are installed at mount: a window keeps the rates it was mounted with until it is opened again.</td></tr><tr><td>Port traffic, refused by kernl&#39;s guard</td><td>kernl&#39;s ingress guard, focus firewall and hidden-window check, enforced by kehto&#39;s runtime firewall</td><td>When <code>observe</code> returns <code>defer</code> or <code>reject</code> (<code>size:&lt;class&gt;</code>, <code>flood:burst</code>, <code>flood:queue-full</code>, the focus firewall&#39;s <code>rate:&lt;class&gt;</code>, or <code>hidden:&lt;class&gt;</code>), <code>createKernl</code> calls <code>refuseCurrentDispatch(bridge.runtime.firewallState, dTag)</code>: the applet&#39;s runtime policy is <code>deny</code> for the rest of that synchronous dispatch, so kehto refuses the envelope, and the previous policy returns in a microtask, before the next port message can arrive. Control envelopes always come back <code>pass</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The runtime keys its rates by the sender capability a message needs, while the guard classifies by what a message does. <code>RUNTIME_OP_CLASSES</code> translates:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Guard class</th><th>Runtime key</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>relay:subscribe</code></td><td><code>relay:read</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>relay:publish</code></td><td><code>relay:write</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>relay:publish:delete</code></td><td>none; deletions share <code>relay:write</code> in the runtime and keep their own bucket only in kernl&#39;s focus firewall, whose rejections are refused by <code>refuseCurrentDispatch</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>outbox:subscribe</code></td><td><code>outbox:read</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>outbox:publish</code></td><td><code>outbox:write</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>resource:bytes</code></td><td><code>resource:fetch</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>storage:write</code></td><td><code>state:write</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>dm:send</code></td><td><code>dm:write</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>intent:invoke</code></td><td><code>intent:write</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>control</code></td><td>none; control envelopes need no capability</td></tr><tr><td><code>generic</code></td><td>the per-applet global rate (<code>setGlobalRate</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Two limits of this arrangement are stated plainly. The runtime firewall never applies an unfocused multiplier: kehto&#39;s <code>ShellAdapter</code> does not forward <code>getFocusContext</code>, so the runtime builds every observation as focused, and <code>FirewallStateContainer</code> has no setter for <code>unfocusedMultiplier</code> either. The profile&#39;s value (<code>0.25</code> in <code>strict</code>) is applied by kernl&#39;s focus firewall, whose rejections kernl turns into runtime refusals. And a hidden window&#39;s port traffic is refused, not deferred: <code>observe</code> cannot hold a port envelope, so the applet must retry once visible. A hook that let the shell defer instead of refuse is tracked upstream as #399 (<a href="./../alpha-status#upstream-gaps">Alpha status</a>).</p><h2 id="dispositions-and-reasons" tabindex="-1">Dispositions and reasons <a class="header-anchor" href="#dispositions-and-reasons" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dispositions and reasons&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>IngressGuard.admit()</code> returns one of four dispositions:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Disposition</th><th>Meaning</th><th>For the window-level <code>hello</code></th><th>For port traffic (refused, never held)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>pass</code></td><td>Within budget.</td><td>Forwarded to the bridge after the visibility gate and the focus firewall.</td><td>Nothing; the envelope is dispatched.</td></tr><tr><td><code>defer</code></td><td>The per-second burst is spent; the envelope is queued.</td><td>Held; <code>drainAll</code> forwards it as tokens refill.</td><td>A <code>flood:burst</code> event; the envelope is not queued and kernl refuses this dispatch (<code>refuseCurrentDispatch</code>). Control envelopes pass.</td></tr><tr><td><code>reject</code></td><td>The queue is full or the envelope is over its size cap.</td><td>Dropped.</td><td><code>flood:queue-full</code>, <code>size:&lt;class&gt;</code>, <code>rate:&lt;class&gt;</code> from the focus firewall, or <code>hidden:&lt;class&gt;</code> for a non-control envelope from a window that is not visible: an event, and the runtime firewall refuses the envelope (<code>refuseCurrentDispatch</code>). Control envelopes are never refused.</td></tr><tr><td><code>quarantine</code></td><td>The window hit the quarantine threshold or the hidden resource allowance, or is still quarantined.</td><td>Dropped, and the window is revoked.</td><td><code>onQuarantine</code> revokes the binding inside the hook, so this envelope is not dispatched, and the frame is removed.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Every disposition other than <code>pass</code>, and every <code>pass</code> the firewall flagged, emits a <code>GuardEvent</code> with a stable prefixed reason. The user sees these events; secrets never appear in them.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Reason</th><th>Emitted by</th><th>With disposition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>flood:burst</code></td><td>ingress guard</td><td><code>defer</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>flood:queue-full</code></td><td>ingress guard</td><td><code>reject</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>flood:quarantine</code></td><td>ingress guard</td><td><code>quarantine</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>flood:hidden-resource</code></td><td>ingress guard</td><td><code>quarantine</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>size:&lt;class&gt;</code> (<code>size:resource:bytes</code>, <code>size:relay:subscribe</code>, <code>size:outbox:subscribe</code>)</td><td>ingress guard</td><td><code>reject</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>defer:queue-full</code></td><td>visibility gate</td><td><code>reject</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>hidden:&lt;class&gt;</code>, a non-control port envelope from a window that is not visible</td><td>guard pipeline (<code>observe</code>)</td><td><code>reject</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>rate:&lt;class&gt;</code></td><td>focus firewall</td><td><code>reject</code>, or <code>flag</code> when the rate&#39;s action is <code>flag</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>matcher:&lt;id&gt;</code>, kehto&#39;s rule id passed through; the guard&#39;s own <code>matcher:size:*</code> matchers cannot fire in the composed shell, because the ingress guard rejects the same envelopes first</td><td>focus firewall</td><td><code>reject</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>policy:deny</code></td><td>focus firewall</td><td><code>reject</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>prompt:&lt;ruleId&gt;</code> (<code>prompt:policy:ask</code>)</td><td>focus firewall</td><td><code>reject</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="why-csp-never-loosens" tabindex="-1">Why CSP never loosens <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-csp-never-loosens" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why CSP never loosens&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The three profiles scale rates and queues. None of them touches the Content Security Policy. A profile is a statement about how much an applet may do through the shell; the CSP is a statement about whether an applet may bypass the shell at all. Loosening a rate limit lets a trusted applet work faster. Loosening the CSP would let it talk to the network directly, which removes the shell&#39;s ability to see, rate-limit and revoke that traffic and so removes every other mitigation on this page at once.</p><p><code>createAppletCsp(profile)</code> therefore accepts the profile, so that callers configure one place, and ignores it. It returns <code>connectOrigins: []</code> and kehto&#39;s zero-network baseline of 14 directives, which <code>renderCsp</code> serialises as:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>default-src &#39;none&#39;; script-src &#39;unsafe-inline&#39; &#39;wasm-unsafe-eval&#39;; style-src &#39;unsafe-inline&#39;; img-src data: blob:; font-src data:; connect-src &#39;none&#39;; worker-src &#39;none&#39;; child-src &#39;none&#39;; frame-src &#39;none&#39;; media-src &#39;none&#39;; object-src &#39;none&#39;; manifest-src &#39;none&#39;; base-uri &#39;none&#39;; form-action &#39;none&#39;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>An applet that needs a network resource asks the <code>resource</code> domain, and the request goes through a <a href="./bridges">bridge</a>. See <code>csp.feature</code>, scenario &quot;the relaxed profile does not loosen the CSP&quot;.</p><h2 id="no-network-path" tabindex="-1">No network path <a class="header-anchor" href="#no-network-path" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;No network path&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>zero-network-sandbox</code> has three parts: the CSP, kehto&#39;s prelude, and kernl&#39;s frame lock.</p><p><strong>kehto&#39;s prelude.</strong> <code>prepareNappletSrcdoc</code> puts it in the frame right after the CSP <code>&lt;meta&gt;</code>, so it runs before any applet code. Before it opens the port to the shell, the prelude takes every global whose name starts with <code>RTC</code>, plus <code>webkitRTCPeerConnection</code>, <code>mozRTCPeerConnection</code>, <code>webkitRTCSessionDescription</code>, <code>mozRTCSessionDescription</code>, <code>webkitRTCIceCandidate</code> and <code>mozRTCIceCandidate</code>, and redefines each as <code>undefined</code>, read-only, non-configurable and non-enumerable. The names still exist, but no value can be put back: <code>delete</code> and assignment leave them <code>undefined</code>. A peer connection would otherwise be a way out the CSP does not cover.</p><p><strong>kernl&#39;s frame lock</strong> (<a href="./../decisions#d-014">D-014</a>). The prelude cleans only the applet&#39;s own realm. A child frame made from <code>srcdoc</code> or <code>about:blank</code> is a fresh realm with a working <code>RTCPeerConnection</code>, and no CSP directive reaches it, because creating it involves no fetch. <code>@kehto/shell</code> 0.29.0 has no switch for this; kehto&#39;s unreleased <code>experimental.nestedContextLock</code> closes it, and <code>@kernl/guard</code> carries an attributed port of that lock until a release ships it. <code>planMount</code> applies all of it to every applet:</p><ul><li><code>assertAppletHasNoFrame</code> scans the verified bytes and refuses markup that contains an <code>iframe</code>, <code>frame</code>, <code>frameset</code>, <code>object</code>, <code>embed</code>, <code>portal</code> or <code>fencedframe</code> element with <code>denied:nested-frame</code>. It parses with the host&#39;s <code>DOMParser</code> and falls back to a lexical match, which may over-reject.</li><li><code>renderFrameLockScript()</code> is inserted immediately after kehto&#39;s prelude, before any applet script. It installs a Trusted Types <code>default</code> policy that refuses frame markup, namespace-prefixed tags included, at every HTML string sink (<code>innerHTML</code>, <code>outerHTML</code>, <code>insertAdjacentHTML</code>, <code>document.write</code>, <code>createContextualFragment</code>, <code>DOMParser</code>), and pins <code>Document.prototype.createElement</code>, <code>createElementNS</code> and <code>DOMImplementation.prototype.createDocument</code> non-configurable so they throw <code>TypeError: denied:nested-frame</code> for a frame tag name.</li><li>The same script runs <code>appletInsertionLock</code>, because creation routes are open-ended (custom element constructors, XSLT, namespace-prefixed XML parsing all mint frames without a factory) but a frame gets a realm only when it is connected. It pins every DOM insertion method (<code>appendChild</code>, <code>insertBefore</code>, <code>replaceChild</code>, <code>append</code>, <code>prepend</code>, <code>replaceChildren</code>, <code>before</code>, <code>after</code>, <code>replaceWith</code>, <code>insertAdjacentElement</code>, <code>moveBefore</code>, <code>Range.insertNode</code>, <code>Range.surroundContents</code>) to refuse a node that is, or contains, a frame element, reading node identity through native getters captured before applet code runs. It also refuses custom elements that extend frame elements and removes <code>XSLTProcessor</code>. Every insertion therefore walks the inserted subtree once.</li><li>The CSP <code>&lt;meta&gt;</code> kehto rendered gains <code>TRUSTED_TYPES_DIRECTIVES</code>, <code>require-trusted-types-for &#39;script&#39;; trusted-types default</code>, so every string sink goes through that policy and no other policy name can be registered. <code>createAppletCsp</code> carries the directives as <code>AppletCsp.trustedTypes</code>. kehto 0.29.0 refuses directives outside its baseline, so <code>planMount</code> appends them to the rendered meta, and refuses with <code>unavailable:frame-lock</code> when kehto&#39;s output is not where it expects it.</li></ul><p><strong>The CSP</strong> refuses everything else: <code>fetch</code> and WebSocket by <code>connect-src &#39;none&#39;</code>, images by <code>img-src data: blob:</code>, frames that would load a URL by <code>frame-src &#39;none&#39;</code> and <code>child-src &#39;none&#39;</code>, and beacons by <code>connect-src</code>. <code>navigator.sendBeacon</code> still returns <code>true</code>, because it only reports that the beacon was queued; the request is refused when the browser sends it, and fails with <code>csp</code>.</p><p><code>tests/e2e/applet-network-sealed.spec.ts</code> proves this in Chromium. It builds an applet frame with kernl&#39;s own <code>planMount</code> (<code>sandbox=&quot;allow-scripts&quot;</code>, a host page carrying <code>KERNL_FRAME_POLICY_META</code>) and checks that the frame lock follows kehto&#39;s prelude and precedes the applet&#39;s script. The applet code then tries each way out, and the spec asserts:</p><ul><li>each of 15 frame-creation routes, plus <code>document.write</code> whole and split across calls, throws <code>TypeError: denied:nested-frame</code>; the four creation routes the factories cannot see (a customized built-in constructed with <code>new</code>, XSLT, and a namespace-prefixed XHTML element brought in with <code>importNode</code> or <code>adoptNode</code>) fail at creation or at insertion; and thirteen insertion routes (every pinned method, a shadow root, a fragment) refuse an unconnected frame;</li><li>no child realm exists, no <code>RTC*</code> constructor is usable in the applet&#39;s realm, and no realm produces a <code>srflx</code> or <code>relay</code> ICE candidate. As a control, when the host page can reach a public STUN server, the spec asserts that it does and that the applet produces no such candidate; when it cannot, the control is skipped;</li><li><code>fetch</code> fails, the WebSocket never opens, every <code>RTC*</code> global is pinned to <code>undefined</code> and cannot be restored, the image does not load, the browser reports a CSP violation for each attempt including the beacon, and no request reaches the network layer: every outbound request fails with <code>csp</code>.</li></ul><p><a href="./../decisions#d-014">D-014</a> records the negative control: the same probe run against a srcdoc built by kehto 0.29.0 alone lets 11 routes escape, each producing a child realm that reports <code>host</code> and <code>srflx</code> candidates.</p><h2 id="what-the-guard-is-not" tabindex="-1">What the guard is not <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-guard-is-not" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the guard is not&quot;">​</a></h2><p>It is not an access control list. Whether an applet may use a domain is a grant, described in <a href="./identity-and-grants">Identity and grants</a>. The guard assumes the grant exists and asks a different question: is this applet abusing what it was granted, right now?</p><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./../packages/guard"><code>@kernl/guard</code></a></li><li><a href="./../how-tos/change-guard-profile">Change the guard profile</a></li><li><a href="./applets-under-kernl">Applets under kernl</a></li></ul>',54)])])}const f=t(s,[["render",a]]);export{p as __pageData,f as default};
